---
module: httpd.handler.download_transfer
review_date: 2025-11-05
reviewed_by: Claude Code (AI Assistant)
status: FIXED

bug_fixed:
  title: "Watcher spinning on client disconnect causing heartbeat timeout crashes"
  severity: critical

  description: |
    When clients disconnected mid-transfer (MPV seeking, Ctrl+C, network drops),
    the download handler would enter an infinite loop causing CPU spinning and
    eventually httpd heartbeat timeout crashes.

  root_cause:
    - Used ->stop instead of ->cancel in ABORT_DOWNLOAD path
    - ->stop pauses watcher but leaves it registered
    - Incomplete cleanup of file handles and session data
    - Closed file handle triggered repeated watcher firing
    - Each watcher callback re-entered ABORT_DOWNLOAD
    - Recursive error loop consumed CPU until heartbeat timeout

  trigger_scenarios:
    - Client disconnects during large file transfer
    - MPV video player seeking (frequent disconnect/reconnect)
    - Network interruptions during transfer
    - Client-side Ctrl+C during download
    - Output buffer fills when client stops reading (backpressure)

  fix_implemented:
    changes:
      - "Changed ->stop to ->cancel to fully destroy watcher"
      - "Added proper file handle cleanup with close()"
      - "Delete session download data to prevent stale references"
      - "Delete watcher reference from session"
      - "Set flush_shutdown flag for connection cleanup"

    critical_pattern: |
      ABORT_DOWNLOAD:
        $event->w->cancel;              # ✓ Destroy watcher completely
        close($download->{'content_fh'}) if fileno($download->{'content_fh'});
        delete $session->{'watcher'}->{'download_handler'};
        delete $session->{'download'};
        $session->{'flush_shutdown'} = 1;
        return 0;

  test_infrastructure:
    scripts:
      - bin/dev/test-httpd-abort-bug: "Simple 1MB abort test"
      - bin/dev/test-forced-abort: "113MB forced abort trigger"
      - bin/dev/test-backpressure: "Client stops reading scenario"
      - bin/dev/test-httpd-blocking: "Heartbeat responsiveness check"
      - bin/dev/test-httpd-spinning: "Watcher spinning detection"
      - bin/dev/switch-httpd-version: "Switch between broken/fixed/test versions"

    module_versions:
      - httpd.handler.download_transfer.fixed: "Production (active)"
      - httpd.handler.download_transfer.broken: "Bug reproduction"
      - httpd.handler.download_transfer.test: "Test with forced abort at 113MB"
      - httpd.handler.download_transfer.test-broken: "Test broken version"

  verification:
    - "All 5 test suites pass"
    - "httpd remains responsive after client disconnect"
    - "No watcher spinning detected"
    - "Clean single abort with proper cleanup"
    - "Production deployment: 2025-11-05"

regression_prevention:
  watch_for:
    - "Any use of ->stop on IO watchers (should use ->cancel)"
    - "Missing cleanup in error paths"
    - "File handle operations without corresponding close()"
    - "Session data not deleted on abort"

  best_practices:
    - "Always use ->cancel to destroy watchers, not ->stop"
    - "Implement complete cleanup in all error paths"
    - "Close file handles before deleting references"
    - "Delete session data to prevent stale references"
    - "Test with client disconnect scenarios"
    - "Monitor heartbeat responsiveness under load"

notes: |
  This bug was particularly insidious because it only manifested under
  production conditions with real clients. The test infrastructure now
  allows reproduction and regression testing. The forced abort at 113MB
  reliably triggers the code path.

  The .test and .test-broken versions should be maintained for future
  regression testing.

